<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-critically-damped-analysis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing a Critically Damped System</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SUMMARY ASSEMBLAGE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We continue analyzing overdamped and critically damped systems.
    Recall the shared behavior of these two cases.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-crit-summary">
    <title>Summary: Overdamped and Critically Damped Cases of <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m></title>
    <tabular halign="left" row-headers="yes">
      <col width="22%"/>
      <col width="36%"/>
      <col width="20%"/>
      <col width="22%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Discriminant and roots</cell>
        <cell>Crossings of <m>x=0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Overdamped</term> (larger <m>c</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 > 4km</m> (two distinct real roots)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Critically damped</term> (borderline)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 = 4km</m> (one repeated real root)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SETUP: ODE AND SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Consider the spring-mass-dashpot system with <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 4</m>,
    <m>k = 4</m>, set in motion at <m>t = 0</m> with <m>x(0) = 1</m> and
    <m>x'(0) = 2</m>. Substituting these parameters gives the IVP:
    <me>x'' + 4x' + 4x = 0, \quad x(0) = 1, \quad x'(0) = 2.</me>
    The solution to this IVP is:
    <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(1 + 4t).</me>
  </p>

  <solution>
    <title>Work: Solution to the IVP (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      With <m>m=1</m>, <m>c=4</m>, <m>k=4</m> the characteristic equation is
      <m>r^2 + 4r + 4 = 0</m>, which factors as <m>(r+2)^2 = 0</m>.
      This gives one repeated root <m>r = -2</m>, confirming the system is
      critically damped. The general solution is:
      <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(c_1 + c_2 t).</me>
      Applying <m>x(0) = 1</m>: <m>c_1 = 1</m>.
      Differentiating:
      <me>x'(t) = -2e^{-2t}(c_1 + c_2 t) + e^{-2t}(c_2).</me>
      Applying <m>x'(0) = 2</m>:
      <me>x'(0) = -2c_1 + c_2 = 2 \implies c_2 = 2 + 2(1) = 4.</me>
      So <m>x(t) = e^{-2t}(1 + 4t)</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: SKETCHING THE GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-critically-damped-sketch">
    <title>Sketching the Graph from Initial Conditions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We continue analyzing the same position function from above,
        <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(1 + 4t),</me>
        using the same method as in <xref ref="exploration-overdamped-analysis"/>
        to build a picture of the motion.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <task xml:id="ex-sketch-task-equil">
      <title>Does the Mass Pass Through Equilibrium?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does the mass ever pass through the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m>
          for <m>t \geq 0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Set <m>x(t) = 0</m> and solve for <m>t</m>. Since <m>e^{-2t} \neq 0</m>,
          focus on the other factor. Remember that only <m>t \geq 0</m> is
          physically meaningful.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We solve <m>e^{-2t}(1 + 4t) = 0</m>.
          Since <m>e^{-2t} \neq 0</m>, we need <m>1 + 4t = 0</m>,
          giving <m>t = -\tfrac{1}{4} \lt 0</m>.
          Since this is not a valid time, the mass <alert>never passes
          through equilibrium</alert> for <m>t \geq 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-sketch-task-initial">
      <title>What Do the Initial Conditions Tell Us?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What do the initial conditions <m>x(0) = 1</m> and <m>x'(0) = 2</m>
          tell us about the behavior of the mass?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          We were given <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m> in the problem setup.
          Are they positive or negative? Recall that <m>x > 0</m> means stretched
          and <m>x \lt 0</m> means compressed, while the sign of <m>x'</m> tells
          us the direction of motion.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>x(0) = 1 > 0</m>, the mass starts <alert>stretched</alert>.
          Since <m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m>, the mass is initially moving in the
          positive direction, meaning it is becoming <alert>even more stretched</alert>
          at first. Together with the result of the previous task, the mass starts
          stretched, moves further from equilibrium, and then approaches zero
          from above without ever crossing it.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-sketch-task-graph">
      <title>Putting It Together</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Combining the two observations above: the mass starts stretched,
          initially moves further from equilibrium, never crosses <m>x = 0</m>,
          and eventually approaches zero from above.
          This gives the following graph.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-critically-damped-sketch">
          <caption>
            Graph of <m>x(t) = e^{-2t}(1+4t)</m>. The mass starts at
            <m>x(0) = 1</m> (stretched), increases further, then decays
            toward zero without ever crossing the equilibrium.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-critically-damped-sketch"
            iframe="ch3-graph/static-critically-damped-graph.html"
            width="100%" aspect="5:2">
            <description>
              Graph of x of t equals e to the negative 2t times the quantity
              1 plus 4t, for t from 0 to 4. The curve starts at x equals 1
              when t equals 0, increases to a peak above 1, then decreases
              and approaches zero from above as t increases. A dashed green
              horizontal line marks the equilibrium at x equals zero. The
              curve stays entirely above zero. A red dot marks the starting
              point at t equals 0, x equals 1.
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: ANSWER QUESTIONS ABOUT THE BEHAVIOR
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="activity-critically-damped-questions">
    <title>Analyzing the Behavior</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        We continue with the same position function,
        <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(1 + 4t).</me>
        Now that we have a complete picture of the motion, we use it to answer
        questions about the system.
        <alert>Make sure to reference the previous example to help answer the
        following questions.</alert>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task 1: Never compressed -->

    <task xml:id="act-task-never-compressed">
      <title>Is the Spring Ever Compressed?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain how you can tell the spring is never compressed.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>x(0) > 0</m> and the mass never crosses the equilibrium position,
              so <m>x(t) > 0</m> for all <m>t \geq 0</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Since <m>x(0) = 1 > 0</m> (starts stretched) and the mass
              never reaches <m>x = 0</m>, it stays on the stretched side for all
              time. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>x'(0) > 0</m>, so the mass is moving away from the compressed region.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The sign of <m>x'(0)</m> tells us the initial direction of motion,
              but alone it does not guarantee the spring is never compressed.
              The key facts are that <m>x(0) > 0</m> and the mass never crosses
              equilibrium.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The system is critically damped, so it cannot become compressed.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The damping case alone does not determine whether the spring is
              compressed. A critically damped system can start compressed and
              remain so. What matters here is the combination of initial conditions
              and the equilibrium crossing result.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 2: Find the critical point -->

    <task xml:id="act-task-find-max">
      <title>Find the Time of Maximum Stretch</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the exact time at which the mass is farthest from the equilibrium position.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The maximum stretch occurs either at:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>t = 0</m>, or</p></li>
            <li><p>a critical point where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>.</p></li>
          </ul>
          Looking back at the graph from the previous example, can you deduce which one it is?
        </p>
      </hint>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m>, the mass is still moving away from equilibrium
          at <m>t = 0</m>, so the maximum stretch does <em>not</em> occur at <m>t = 0</m>.
          It must occur at a critical point where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>, so we compute
          <m>x'(t)</m> and solve.
        </p>

        <solution>
          <title>Step 1: Compute <m>x'(t)</m></title>
          <p>
            Using the product rule on <m>x(t) = e^{-2t}(1 + 4t)</m>:
            <md>
              <mrow>x'(t) \amp= -2e^{-2t}(1 + 4t) + e^{-2t}(4)</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= e^{-2t}(-2 - 8t + 4)</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= e^{-2t}(2 - 8t).</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>

        <p>
          So <m>x'(t) = e^{-2t}(2 - 8t)</m>. Now we set this equal to zero and solve.
        </p>

        <solution>
          <title>Step 2: Solve <m>x'(t) = 0</m></title>
          <p>
            We need to solve:
            <me>e^{-2t}(2 - 8t) = 0.</me>
            Since <m>e^{-2t} \neq 0</m>, we need:
            <me>2 - 8t = 0 \implies t = \frac{1}{4}.</me>
            So the maximum stretch occurs at <m>t = \tfrac{1}{4}</m>,
            where <m>x\!\left(\tfrac{1}{4}\right) = e^{-1/2}(1 + 1) = 2e^{-1/2} \approx 1.21</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>

        <p>
          The graph below shows the maximum stretch marked at <m>t = \tfrac{1}{4}</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-critically-damped-max">
          <caption>
            The maximum stretch occurs at <m>t = \tfrac{1}{4}</m>,
            where <m>x = 2e^{-1/2} \approx 1.21</m>.
            After this point the mass moves back toward equilibrium.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-critically-damped-max"
            iframe="ch3-graph/static-critically-damped-graph-max.html"
            width="100%" aspect="5:2">
            <description>
              Graph of x of t equals e to the negative 2t times the quantity
              1 plus 4t. The curve starts at x equals 1 at t equals 0,
              rises to a maximum at t equals one quarter where x equals
              2 e to the negative one half, approximately 1.21, then decreases
              toward zero from above. The maximum point is marked with a filled
              red circle labeled max stretch. A vertical dashed red line drops
              from the maximum to the t axis, where t equals one quarter is
              labeled. The starting point x equals 1 is marked in blue.
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Finding the farthest point from equilibrium:</alert>
          this always happens either at <m>t = 0</m> or at a critical point
          where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>How to tell which one:</alert> you can determine this from a
          sketch of the graph. In particular, check the sign of <m>x'(0)</m>:
          if the mass is moving <em>toward</em> equilibrium at <m>t = 0</m>,
          then <m>t = 0</m> is already the farthest point. But if the mass is
          moving <em>away</em> from equilibrium, as in this example
          (<m>x'(0) = 2 > 0</m> on the stretched side), then it gets even
          farther before turning around, so the farthest point occurs at a
          later critical point, which we find by solving <m>x'(t) = 0</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Note for underdamped systems:</alert> the same logic applies,
          but since underdamped solutions oscillate, there are infinitely many
          critical points, one at each direction change. The farthest point from
          equilibrium is still either <m>t = 0</m> or the first critical point,
          but the mass continues changing direction forever after that.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="video-critically-damped-analysis">
    <caption>This video goes through the content of this page.</caption>
    <video youtube="AGjmBNuJSvU" width="100%" preview="default"/>
  </figure>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-4-4"
    iframe="question-form.html?section=3.4.4"
    width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</section>
